<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Territories of the Gold Coast</h1><p class="st-meta">1901 CE – 1957 CE · capital Tamale</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate over the savanna lands north of Ashanti, which supplied labour to the colony's south and became the north of Ghana in 1957.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 1890s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> raced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for the lands north of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>, signing treaties with rulers such as those of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mamprugu">Mamprugu</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gonja">Kingdom of Gonja</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wala-state">Wala State</a>. The boundaries were fixed by agreements of 1898-1899, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was formally established in 1901, with its capital later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamale">Tamale</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> ran the territory cheaply, through chiefs and a few officials. It was treated mainly as a reservoir of labour for the gold mines and cocoa farms of the south, and as a recruiting ground for the army. Mission activity and schooling were limited, so the north entered the late colonial period far behind the south in education and infrastructure.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 6 March 1957 the Northern Territories became part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ashanti-colony">Ashanti Colony</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-togoland">British Togoland</a>. The gap between north and south remains a theme of Ghanaian politics.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/northern-territories-of-the-gold-coast.jpg" alt="Northern Territories of the Gold Coast" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(1-2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1901 CE – 1957 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tamale</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dagbani, Mampruli, Gonja, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ghana</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Northern Territories Protectorate</dd></dl></aside></div>
